        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#assess" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Assess What You Have</a>
          <a href="#water-test" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Water Test</a>
          <a href="#repairs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Repairs and Fasteners</a>
          <a href="#cleaner-chemistry" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Which Cleaner You Need</a>
          <a href="#washing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Washing the Deck</a>
          <a href="#brightening" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Brightening</a>
          <a href="#sanding" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Sanding, Only Where Needed</a>
          <a href="#drying" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Drying Before You Stain</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Preparation is not about making the deck look clean. It is about removing every layer that would sit between the stain and the wood, and then getting the wood dry enough to accept what you put on it. Everything below serves those two goals.
        </p>

        <div id="assess" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Assess What You Actually Have
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no single preparation routine, because decks arrive at this point in very different states. Walk yours and answer three questions before spending anything.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You See</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Needs</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Uniform silvery grey, no coating left</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean and brighten only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Black or green patches, mostly shaded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mildew cleaner, then brighten</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Patchy colour, some areas still coated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip, then clean and brighten</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling or flaking film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip completely; a recoat will fail</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fuzzy, furred surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean, then sand at 60–80 grit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">New, smooth, slightly shiny boards</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mill glaze: clean, water test, sand if it beads</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Black streaks around screws</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tannin or iron reaction: brightener lifts it</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Most decks fall into the first two rows, which is good news: cleaning and brightening is a Saturday, not a project. The rows that change the schedule are the ones involving a film that is still present, because stripping adds a day and a genuinely unpleasant hour or two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Water Test, and What It Is Really Telling You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sprinkle a tablespoon of water onto the boards in five or six places: a sunny spot, a shaded spot, a walkway, under where a chair sat, and near the house. Watch for a minute or two.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Soaks in and darkens the wood.</strong> The surface is open. Once it is clean and dry, stain will penetrate.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Beads and sits.</strong> Something is repelling it — an old finish, mill glaze, or wood that is still saturated. Stain will not penetrate here.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Different results in different places.</strong> The most important finding of all, and the reason to test in more than one spot.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            That last case is what produces blotchy finishes. A deck where the walkway has worn bare while the sheltered edges still hold their old coating will absorb stain at two different rates and dry two different colours. Preparation has to bring the whole surface to the same condition, which usually means stripping the areas that still repel rather than hoping the difference will even out. It will not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repeat this test at the end of preparation, before you open the stain. It is the same test doing a different job: first it tells you what you are dealing with, then it tells you whether you are ready.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repairs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Repairs, Fasteners and the Gaps Between Boards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Do the carpentry before the chemistry. Set any protruding nail heads or screws below the surface, replace fasteners that are rusting badly, and swap out boards that are split, soft or cupped enough to hold standing water. A new board can be cleaned and stained with the rest, and it is far less work now than after the deck is finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear the gaps between the boards with a putty knife or a thin scraper. The compacted debris in those gaps holds moisture against board edges around the clock, and edge rot is a common way decking fails while the faces still look fine. It also blocks drainage, which matters when you rinse the deck later.
          </p>

          <p>
            While you are down there, look at the structure. Check the ledger board where the deck attaches to the house, probe any dark or soft spots with a screwdriver, and look for corrosion on hangers and connectors. None of this is part of staining, but it is the one time a year you have the deck bare and empty, and structural problems only get more expensive from here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaner-chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Cleaner You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            "Deck cleaner" covers several different chemistries that do different jobs, and buying the wrong one wastes a weekend.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Is For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium percarbonate cleaner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mildew, dirt and grey fibres, without damaging wood</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium hydroxide stripper</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Removing an intact or peeling old coating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oxalic acid brightener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Neutralising cleaners, lifting tannin and rust marks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chlorine bleach</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Kills mildew but damages fibres — not recommended</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The bleach line is worth dwelling on, because it is the traditional advice and it is wrong for this job. Household chlorine bleach kills surface mildew, but it also breaks down lignin and cellulose, leaches natural colour out of the wood, corrodes fasteners, and leaves the grey oxidised layer exactly where it was. Percarbonate cleaners — often sold as oxygen bleach — remove the grey as well as the mildew without the fibre damage. Our <a href="/best-deck-cleaner/">deck cleaner testing</a> covers the practical differences between products.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the assessment above put you in the stripping category, that is a distinct process with its own hazards and technique, and it is covered in detail in <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">how to strip deck stain</a>. The short version is that a stripper removes the coating and a cleaner removes what is on the wood, and a deck with a failing solid stain needs both, in that order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="washing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Washing the Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wet the boards down first so the cleaner spreads evenly rather than soaking straight into dry wood in patches. Wet the plants around the deck as well, and rinse them again afterwards. Then apply the cleaner in manageable sections — a section you can scrub and rinse before the chemical dries is the right size, which on a hot day may be smaller than you expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let it dwell for the label time, usually ten to fifteen minutes, keeping it damp with a light mist if the sun is drying it out. Then scrub along the grain with a stiff synthetic brush on a pole. This scrubbing is what actually removes the loosened grey layer; the chemical alone only softens it. Rinse thoroughly, and be generous with the rinse — cleaner residue left on the wood interferes with the stain later.
          </p>

          <p>
            On pressure washers, the honest advice is that most decks do not need one and many are damaged by one. If you use one, treat it as a rinsing tool rather than a cutting tool: under about 1,500 psi, a 25 or 40 degree fan tip, kept moving, held a foot or more from the surface, working with the grain. The furred surface a too-aggressive wash leaves behind absorbs stain unevenly and weathers faster, and it commits you to sanding afterwards. Our guide to <a href="/what-psi-pressure-washer-do-i-need/">choosing a pressure washer PSI</a> covers the numbers in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brightening" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Brightening: The Step Most People Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cleaners and strippers are alkaline, and they leave the wood surface alkaline after rinsing. That matters for three reasons: alkaline wood darkens, particularly cedar and redwood; it can react with some stains; and the raised pH keeps the grain partially closed. A brightener — almost always oxalic acid based — reverses all three in about twenty minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply it to the still-damp deck, let it dwell for the stated time, agitate lightly with the brush, and rinse well. The colour change is immediate and dramatic on a deck that has just been cleaned, which is why the product is called a brightener, but the appearance is a side effect rather than the point. The point is neutral pH and open grain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brightener is also the fix for the black streaks that appear around fasteners on cedar, redwood and other tannin-rich woods. Those marks are a reaction between the wood's natural tannins and iron, and oxalic acid lifts them where scrubbing will not. If your deck has them, do not skip this step and do not stain over them — they will show through everything except a solid colour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sanding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Sanding, Only Where It Is Needed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sanding an entire deck is hard work and usually unnecessary. Sand where there is a specific problem to solve: raised fibres after an aggressive wash, mill glaze on new smooth lumber that still beads water after cleaning, splinters on handrails and stair treads, or residue left behind after stripping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use 60 to 80 grit and stop there. This is counter-intuitive to anyone used to finishing interior woodwork, where finer is better, but exterior stain needs an open surface to soak into. Sanding to 120 or beyond burnishes the wood, closes the pores, and produces a deck that rejects stain in exactly the way you were trying to fix. A random orbit sander or a pole sander does the flat work; a sanding sponge handles railings and edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean up thoroughly afterwards. Sanding dust sitting in the grain is another layer between the stain and the wood, and it is easy to leave behind because it is the same colour as the deck. Vacuum, then blow or sweep, then check the gaps between boards again. If you sanded after brightening, a light rinse and another drying period is sensible before staining.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Drying, and Why It Takes Longer Than You Think
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood dries from the outside in. That single fact explains most of the failures in this category. Twelve hours after washing, the surface can feel bone dry while the boards hold enough moisture a few millimetres down to stop stain penetrating and, in some cases, to cloud an oil-based finish as it tries to cure over trapped water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan on 24 hours minimum after a light wash and 48 or more after a pressure wash, and extend both in humid, cool or shaded conditions. A deck in deep shade in autumn can need three or four days. There is no penalty for waiting and a real penalty for not, so if the weather gives you an extra dry day before staining, take it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The water test settles it. Sprinkle water in several places, including the shadiest and slowest-drying part of the deck, and only proceed when it soaks in everywhere. If you want a number rather than a judgement, a moisture meter reading below roughly 15 percent is the usual guidance, though the test is genuinely reliable enough for a homeowner. Once you are through this step, the actual staining is covered in <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/">how to stain a deck</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">New Decks and Mill Glaze</h2>
        <p>
          A brand new deck looks like the cleanest surface you will ever stain, and it frequently is the least receptive. Planing and milling burnish the wood surface, crushing and sealing the outer cells into what is known as mill glaze, and that glassy layer repels finish exactly the way an old sealer would. Add the dirt picked up in the lumber yard and, on treated boards, the moisture from the treatment process itself, and a new deck often needs more preparation than a weathered one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The sequence is the same but the emphasis shifts. Clean it to remove yard dirt and any surface residue, then run the water test. If water soaks in, you are ready once the boards are dry. If it beads, the choice is between letting the deck weather for a few months, which breaks down the glaze naturally, or sanding at 60 to 80 grit to cut through it. Both work; weathering is free and sanding is fast.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pressure-treated lumber adds a second variable, which is the water still in the wood from the treatment plant. It is entirely possible for new treated boards to be too wet to stain for weeks or months after installation, and no amount of preparation changes that — it just needs time. Our guide to <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/">staining pressure-treated wood</a> covers how to judge it, and <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-pressure-treated-wood/">the stains that work best on it</a> covers what to use when it is finally ready.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Safety and Protecting Everything Nearby</h2>
        <p>
          Deck strippers and cleaners are genuinely caustic. Sodium hydroxide strippers burn skin and damage eyes on contact, and even the milder percarbonate cleaners are unpleasant to get in a cut or an eye. Chemical-resistant gloves, sealed eye protection and long sleeves are the minimum, and closed shoes matter more than people expect on a wet deck covered in slippery chemistry.
        </p>
        <p>
          Plants are the most common casualty. Wet the surrounding foliage before you start, cover what you can with plastic sheeting, and rinse everything down thoroughly when you finish each section rather than at the end of the day. Grass immediately below a raised deck takes the runoff from every rinse and is worth soaking well before and after.
        </p>
        <p>
          Protect the house too. Mask siding adjacent to the deck, particularly vinyl, which some strippers will mark, and cover any metal or composite railing components that are not being coated. Aluminium and anodised finishes react badly to strong alkaline cleaners. Ten minutes of masking saves a repair that no amount of staining will hide.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">How Much Preparation Is Enough?</h2>
        <p>
          There is a point of diminishing returns, and it is worth naming so that the job stays proportionate. Preparation is finished when the entire deck surface accepts water at roughly the same rate, when there is no loose fibre or residue on the boards, and when the wood is dry through. It does not need to look new, and chasing an even colour with more cleaning usually just removes more wood.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some marks are permanent and will show through anything but a solid stain: deep iron staining, weathering that has cut into the grain, mismatched replacement boards, and old paint drips ground into the surface. Recognising these early lets you make the right decision about opacity rather than sanding a deck to death trying to remove them. The trade-off between hiding flaws and showing grain is covered in <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/">solid versus semi-transparent stain</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also worth remembering that preparation done well makes the next one easier. A deck that was properly cleaned, brightened and stained with a penetrating product will wear evenly and need only a wash before its next coat, which is a Saturday rather than a project. A deck that was stained over a half-prepared surface fails unevenly and drags you back into stripping. The effort compounds in whichever direction you send it.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Brightener</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cleaners leave the wood alkaline, which darkens some species, keeps the grain partially closed and can interfere with the stain. Brightening takes twenty minutes, neutralises the surface, and lifts the black tannin marks around fasteners that nothing else will remove.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Chlorine Bleach</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It kills mildew but breaks down wood fibres, leaches natural colour, corrodes fasteners and leaves the grey oxidised layer untouched. A sodium percarbonate cleaner removes the grey along with the mildew and does not damage the wood, which is why it is the standard now.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing Too Aggressively</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High pressure at close range cuts into softwood, raises the fibres and leaves a furry surface that drinks stain unevenly and weathers faster. Stay under about 1,500 psi with a wide tip held well back, and accept that a badly washed deck now needs sanding as well.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding to Too Fine a Grit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Exterior stain needs an open surface to soak into. Sanding past about 80 grit burnishes the wood and closes the pores, so the stain sits on top and wears away early. Finer is better indoors and actively harmful on a deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining the Day After Washing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boards dry from the outside in, so a surface that feels dry can still hold enough moisture underneath to stop penetration and cloud an oil finish. Twenty-four hours is a minimum, forty-eight after pressure washing, and longer when it is humid, cool or shaded.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you prepare a deck for staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clear and repair it, strip any failing coating, wash it with a deck cleaner to remove mildew and grey fibres, neutralise with a brightener, sand only where the surface is rough, then let it dry until water soaks in rather than beading.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to strip my deck before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if a previous coating is still present. A worn penetrating stain that has faded away needs cleaning, not stripping. A solid stain or paint that is peeling, or any finish that still makes water bead, has to come off before anything new will bond.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between deck cleaner and deck brightener?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A cleaner is alkaline and removes mildew, dirt and grey fibres. A brightener is acidic, usually oxalic acid, and neutralises that alkalinity while lifting tannin and rust marks. They are two halves of one process, and the cleaner alone leaves the wood chemically unbalanced.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you sand a deck before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only where it is needed: raised fibres after pressure washing, mill glaze on new lumber, splinters on handrails, or residue after stripping. Use 60 to 80 grit and no finer, because sanding too smooth closes the grain and reduces absorption.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know when a deck is dry enough to stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sprinkle water on several spots including the shadiest. If it soaks in and darkens the wood within a minute or two, the deck is ready. If it beads or sits, either the wood is still wet or an old finish is still working.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use bleach to clean a deck before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It kills surface mildew but damages wood fibres, strips natural colour, can corrode fasteners, and leaves the grey oxidised layer behind. Sodium percarbonate cleaners do the same job without the fibre damage and are the better choice before staining.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after cleaning a deck can you stain it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally 24 hours after a light wash and 48 or more after pressure washing, extended in humid, cool or shaded conditions. Boards dry from the outside in, so the surface can feel dry while the wood beneath is not. Trust the water test over the clock.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to prep a brand new deck before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. New lumber carries mill glaze from planing, dirt from the yard, and often moisture from treatment, all of which stop stain penetrating. Clean it, then run the water test. If it beads, it needs more weathering time or a light sanding.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

