        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Preparation exists to do two things: remove everything sitting between the stain and the wood, and get the wood dry enough to accept it. Assess the deck first, because the routine changes depending on whether an old coating is still present — cleaning and brightening is a Saturday, stripping is a project.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Use a percarbonate cleaner rather than chlorine bleach, always follow it with an oxalic acid brightener, and sand only where there is a specific problem to fix, at 60 to 80 grit and no finer. Each of those choices is about keeping the grain open so the stain can get into it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then wait longer than feels necessary. The water test is the only reliable go-ahead: when water soaks in everywhere on the deck, including the shadiest corner, the surface is genuinely ready. That single check prevents most of the blotchy, short-lived finishes people blame on the stain.
          </p>
        </div>


